'''alter''' (âl′tėr), ''v.'' [< ML. ''alterare'', make other, < L. ''alter'', other, < ''al-'' (seen in ''alius'', other, ''alienus'', of another, etc.: see ''alias'', ''alien'', etc.)

readings.c3s2:
+ compar. suffix ''-ter'' = E. ''-ther'' in ''other'', ''whether'', etc., and ''-ter'' in ''after'', etc.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To make some change in; make different in some particular; cause to vary in some degree, without an entire change.
{{smaller block|My covenant will I not break, nor ''alter'' the thing that is gone out of my lips.
{{right|Ps. lxxxix. 34.}}
These things are to be regretted, but not to be ''altered'' until liberality of sentiment is more universal.
{{right|''Washington'', in Bancroft’s Hist. Const., I. 443.}}
There are speeches, some speeches of Demosthenes particularly, in which it would be impossible to ''alter'' a word without ''altering'' it for the worse.
{{right|''Macaulay'', History.}}}}
'''2.''' To change entirely or materially; convert into another form or state: as, to ''alter'' a cloak into a coat; to ''alter'' an opinion.
{{smaller block|<poem>She promised that no force,
Persuasion, no, nor death could ''alter'' her.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Aylmer’s Field.}}}}
'''3.''' To castrate, emasculate, or spay, as an animal. [United States.]—'''4†.''' To exchange.
{{smaller block|She that would ''alter'' services with thee.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., ii. 5.}}}}
'''5†.''' To agitate: as, “''altered'' and moved inwardly,” ''Milton'', Areopagitica, p. 1. ='''Syn. 1''' {{smaller|and '''2.''' ''Alter'', ''Change'', modify, transform, transmute. In general ''alter'' is to change partially, while ''change'' is more commonly to substitute one thing for another, or to make a material difference in a thing.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I woo thee not with gifts.
Sequel of guerdon could not ''alter'' me
To fairer.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Œnone.}}
<poem>One who brings
A mind not to be ''changed'' by place or time.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 253.}}}}

